INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'nice-layoffs-500-ai-transition-20261103', 'global', 'Israel',
'Nice Layoffs: 500 Jobs Cut as AI Reshapes Customer Service',
'',
'Nice began layoffs affecting about 500 employees worldwide on 3 November 2026, about 5 per cent of its 10,000-strong workforce, as AI tools reshape the customer service work its software supports.',
'',
$$<p>On 3 November 2026, the Israeli-founded software company Nice began a round of layoffs affecting about 500 employees worldwide, as artificial intelligence continues to change the customer service industry it sells into. The Nice layoffs, reported by CTech on 3 November 2026, amount to about 5 per cent of the company's global workforce of 10,000, and they arrive alongside a wider reshaping of the business that includes selling one division and buying an AI-agent start-up. Nice said the step was needed to position the company for the future.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Employer</th><td>Nice (Nice Ltd), customer service and customer relationship software</td></tr>
<tr><th>Jobs affected</th><td>About 500 worldwide, about 5 per cent of a workforce of about 10,000</td></tr>
<tr><th>Employees in Israel</th><td>About 1,000 of the global workforce; estimates cited by CTech put little impact on operations in Israel</td></tr>
<tr><th>Reason given</th><td>Organisational restructuring to serve customers adopting AI-driven customer experience tools at enterprise scale</td></tr>
<tr><th>Roles or countries affected</th><td>Not named in the report</td></tr>
<tr><th>Severance or timetable</th><td>Not stated in the report</td></tr>
<tr><th>Third-quarter results due</th><td>Wednesday, 4 November 2026</td></tr>
</table>
<h2>What Nice said about the Nice layoffs</h2>
<p>Nice confirmed the scale of the cuts in a statement quoted by CTech. The company said it was making changes to its organisational structure and resource allocation to better serve customers implementing AI-driven customer experience solutions at enterprise scale. It added that it anticipated reducing its global workforce by approximately 5 per cent, that the step was necessary to position Nice for the future and meet its strategic goals, and that the decisions involved valued colleagues. The statement put direct conversations with affected employees, and full support through the transition, as the immediate priority.</p>
<p>That framing matters because it ties the cuts directly to AI, rather than to a general downturn alone. Nice sells software that helps companies run contact centres and customer relationships, and its customers are now buying AI tools that automate parts of that work. Nice is not alone: <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/us-job-cuts-october-2026-ai-153074-20261103">US employers announced 153,074 job cuts in October 2026</a>, with technology among the hardest-hit sectors and AI cited as a stated reason for 31,039 of those cuts.</p>
<h2>Why the cuts are happening now</h2>
<p>CTech reported that Nice has reached a crossroads over the past two years, a period that matches the tenure of chief executive Scott Russell and the upheaval brought by artificial intelligence. The company's share price has fallen 17 per cent over the past year. Nice is now valued at about $6.5 billion, well below the roughly $20 billion peak it reached during the Covid-19 pandemic.</p>
<p>Trading conditions have also been difficult. After its previous quarterly results, Nice management said customers had delayed signing contracts during the first two quarters of 2026, and that even some of its largest clients, including major financial institutions, had chosen to wait and assess how the AI market develops before committing to significant spending. The company expects contract signings to accelerate in the second half of the year, but that recovery has not yet removed the need to cut costs. Alongside the layoffs, Nice is seeking to sell its Actimize division while expanding its AI capabilities through the $1 billion acquisition of a German AI-agent start-up, whose technology is intended to help customers automate service operations and reduce their reliance on human call-centre staff.</p>
<h2>The Israel question, and the numbers behind the business</h2>
<p>One detail in the report will be watched closely in Israel. CTech noted that chief executive Scott Russell had promised in July 2026 that the company would not reduce its workforce in Israel, yet the company is now initiating layoffs worldwide. The report added that, according to estimates, the cuts will have little impact on the company's operations in Israel, where it employs about 1,000 people. The report did not name which roles or countries are affected.</p>
<p>The financial backdrop shows a company still growing in revenue terms but under profit pressure. Nice is on track to pass $3 billion in annual revenue for the first time, assuming it meets its forecast for 10 per cent growth. Earnings per share, however, are expected to fall by nearly a dollar, to about $11, despite an aggressive share buyback programme. For the third quarter, the company has forecast revenue of $780 million to $790 million and earnings of $2.73 to $2.83 per share, with results due on Wednesday, 4 November 2026.</p>
<h2>What this means for customer service workers</h2>
<p>For workers in customer service, support and contact-centre roles, the Nice layoffs are a warning and a pointer at the same time. The warning is plain: employers are now willing to name AI as a reason for cutting roles, and the cuts are reaching software vendors as well as the companies that buy their tools. The pointer is equally plain: the jobs that remain, and the jobs being created, cluster around building, supervising and selling AI-driven service, including configuring AI agents, checking their answers, handling the cases they cannot resolve, and supporting the systems for enterprise clients.</p>
<h2>What to do next</h2>
<p>If you work at Nice, wait for the direct conversation the company has promised before acting on rumours about which teams are affected, and keep a copy of your contract, recent performance records and any written notice you receive. If you work in customer service elsewhere, list the tasks an AI agent already handles in your team, the tasks that still need a person, and the skills you would need to move towards the second list.</p>
<p>Jobseekers should also look at where hiring is still strong. AI-focused employers continue to recruit for roles that build and deploy these systems, including in Singapore, where <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/slalom-singapore-hub-ai-jobs-20261103">Slalom opened a Southeast Asia hub in November 2026 hiring across cloud, engineering, data and AI</a>. The contrast is the point: AI is cutting some service jobs while creating others. Finally, treat any recruiter who asks for payment to secure a role as a scam: genuine employers do not charge candidates to apply.</p>
<p><small>Sources: CTech report on the Nice layoffs, published 3 November 2026.</small></p>$$,
'',
ARRAY['https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/selqjgkso'],
NOW(), NOW(), NOW())
ON CONFLICT ("slug") DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'eps-korea-language-tests-76173-applicants-20261103', 'global', 'Nepal',
'EPS Korea Language Tests Begin 17 November for 76,173 Applicants',
'',
'Nepal News reported on 3 November 2026 that 76,173 applicants will take the EPS Korea language test, with service-sector tests beginning on 17 November 2026 and the window running to 25 February 2027.',
'',
$$<p>On 3 November 2026, Nepal News reported that 76,173 applicants are set to take the Korean language examination under the Employment Permit System (EPS), with tests beginning on 17 November 2026 and running through 25 February 2027. The EPS Korea language test is the gateway for Nepali workers hoping to take up jobs in South Korea through the government-to-government system, and this cycle adds two new sectors, service and forestry, alongside the established manufacturing and agriculture and livestock sectors. Candidates should now check their sector and shift in the official schedule, because the test dates differ sharply by sector.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Examination</th><td>Korean language test under the Employment Permit System (EPS), EPS Korea Branch</td></tr>
<tr><th>Total applicants</th><td>76,173</td></tr>
<tr><th>Service sector tests begin</th><td>17 November 2026</td></tr>
<tr><th>Forestry sector tests begin</th><td>18 November 2026</td></tr>
<tr><th>Agriculture and livestock sector tests begin</th><td>20 November 2026</td></tr>
<tr><th>Manufacturing sector tests begin</th><td>11 December 2026</td></tr>
<tr><th>Test window ends</th><td>25 February 2027</td></tr>
<tr><th>Official schedule source</th><td>EPS Korea Section, epsnepal.gov.np</td></tr>
</table>
<h2>EPS Korea language test: applicants by sector</h2>
<p>The sector breakdown reported by Nepal News adds up exactly to the headline total. Manufacturing accounts for the large majority, with 53,103 applicants, followed by agriculture and livestock with 20,907. The two new sectors are far smaller in this cycle, forestry with 1,124 applicants and service with 1,039, but their addition matters: for the first time in this cycle, Nepali applicants have a formal EPS route into service and forestry work in South Korea, rather than only into factories and farms.</p>
<p>The staggered start dates are equally important. Service-sector candidates go first, on 17 November 2026, followed by forestry on 18 November 2026 and agriculture and livestock on 20 November 2026. Manufacturing candidates, the largest group, do not begin until 11 December 2026. With the window running to 25 February 2027, the programme spans more than three months, so candidates must not assume that a date mentioned for one sector applies to another. Each candidate's individual date and shift are set out in the official schedule documents, which the EPS Korea Section directs candidates to download rather than rely on a summary.</p>
<h2>How this cycle fits the EPS route to South Korea</h2>
<p>The EPS system works through a government-to-government agreement between Nepal and South Korea, which is why it remains one of the most trusted routes for Nepali workers heading abroad. South Korea's employers request workers, Nepal's EPS Korea Branch runs the language test, and successful candidates enter a roster from which Korean employers select workers. Passing the language test does not by itself guarantee a job: roster registration, employer selection and further employment formalities still follow, and candidates should be wary of anyone who promises a placement in exchange for extra payment.</p>
<p>This cycle follows the application round reported earlier in 2026, when <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/south-korea-to-hire-7-200-nepalis-in-2026-under-eps-20261015">South Korea sought 7,200 Nepali workers under EPS, including 5,000 in manufacturing, 2,000 in agriculture and livestock, and 100 each in service and forestry</a>. The gap between 7,200 places sought and 76,173 applicants sitting the test shows how competitive the route is: many more people take the language test than will receive job offers in a single cycle. That competition is also why preparation and accurate paperwork matter. A candidate who passes the test but has a document problem at the roster stage can lose time that a better-prepared candidate does not.</p>
<p>South Korea remains one of the most popular destinations for Nepali migrant workers. Earlier reporting on Growentix noted that <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/125-000-nepalis-secure-jobs-in-south-korea-through-eps-20261011">125,000 Nepalis had secured jobs in South Korea through EPS</a>, a figure that underlines how established the system has become. Candidates should still follow the official timetable closely and keep their contact details up to date with the EPS branch.</p>
<h2>What candidates should check before their test date</h2>
<p>First, confirm your sector. Service, forestry, agriculture and livestock, and manufacturing all start on different dates, and turning up on the wrong date is an avoidable error. Second, download the official schedule for your sector from epsnepal.gov.np and check your examination date, shift and reporting instructions. Nepal News reported the sector start dates, but the shift-level detail lives in the schedule documents, and only the official version should be trusted if a social media post disagrees with it.</p>
<p>Third, prepare the documents the EPS process requires and check that your personal details match your application exactly. Name spelling, date of birth and application number mismatches cause delays at examination centres. Fourth, plan travel and accommodation early, especially for manufacturing candidates whose tests begin in December 2026, when demand for transport and lodging around test centres rises. Candidates travelling from outside Kathmandu Valley should allow a full day of margin.</p>
<h2>What to do next, and the scam caution</h2>
<p>Use only the official channel for everything in this cycle: the EPS Korea Section website, epsnepal.gov.np, and notices from the Department of Foreign Employment. Do not pay any agent who claims to guarantee a test pass, a roster place or a job offer, and do not hand over your passport or application login to a third party. Under the EPS system, recruitment runs government to government, without private agents in the selection chain, so a payment demand is itself a warning sign. If you are unsure about a message claiming to come from the EPS branch, check it against the official website before replying.</p>
<p>For candidates who do not pass, or who pass but are not selected in this cycle, the result is not the end of the route. Later cycles have reopened the same sectors, so keep your study materials and watch for the next official notice rather than relying on agents' promises. For the 76,173 candidates in this cycle, the immediate task is simpler: confirm the sector date, download the official schedule, and prepare for a test that begins for service-sector candidates on 17 November 2026.</p>
<p><small>Sources: Nepal News report on the EPS Korea language tests, published in the 3 November 2026 evening briefing; EPS Korea Section schedule notice of 13 October 2026 via epsnepal.gov.np.</small></p>$$,
'',
ARRAY['https://english.nepalnews.com/s/nation/eps-korea-language-tests-to-begin-november-17-with-two-new-sectors-added/', 'https://epsnepal.gov.np'],
NOW(), NOW(), NOW())
ON CONFLICT ("slug") DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'supreme-court-junior-court-attendant-130-posts-20261104', 'global', 'India',
'Supreme Court Junior Court Attendant Recruitment 2026: 130 Posts, Apply from 4 November',
'',
'The Supreme Court of India opens applications on 4 November 2026 for 130 Junior Court Attendant (Cooking) posts, open to Class 10 pass candidates with a one-year cooking diploma; the window closes on 25 November 2026.',
'',
$$<p>On 4 November 2026, the Supreme Court of India opens online applications for 130 Junior Court Attendant posts, giving Class 10 pass candidates with a cooking qualification a route into the country's highest court. The Supreme Court Junior Court Attendant recruitment 2026, advertised as No. F.3/2026-SC (RC) on 15 October 2026, runs from 4 November 2026 to 25 November 2026, closing at 11:55 PM on the last date. The posts are tentative, so the final number can change, and there is no offline or postal form.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Recruiting body</th><td>Supreme Court of India, Registry, New Delhi</td></tr>
<tr><th>Post</th><td>Junior Court Attendant (Group 'C', non-clerical, cooking)</td></tr>
<tr><th>Total posts</th><td>130 (tentative)</td></tr>
<tr><th>Advertisement</th><td>No. F.3/2026-SC (RC), 15 October 2026</td></tr>
<tr><th>Application window</th><td>4 November 2026 to 25 November 2026, up to 11:55 PM</td></tr>
<tr><th>Pay</th><td>Pay Level 3, Rs. 21,700 to Rs. 69,100; starting basic Rs. 21,700 plus allowances</td></tr>
<tr><th>Qualification</th><td>Class 10 minimum, Class 12 maximum, plus one-year Cooking/Culinary Arts diploma</td></tr>
<tr><th>Age limit</th><td>18 to 27 years as on 1 October 2026, with relaxations</td></tr>
<tr><th>Application fee</th><td>Rs. 1,500 General; Rs. 750 reserved categories, ex-servicemen, candidates with disabilities</td></tr>
<tr><th>Selection</th><td>Objective test of 100 questions, then an interview of 25 marks</td></tr>
<tr><th>How to apply</th><td>Online only, through sci.gov.in</td></tr>
<tr><th>Exam date</th><td>Not announced in the roundup summary checked</td></tr>
</table>
<h2>Supreme Court Junior Court Attendant recruitment 2026: who can apply</h2>
<p>The eligibility rules are narrower than the headline "Class 10 pass" suggests, and candidates should read them before paying the fee. The court said candidates must have passed Class 10 from a recognised board or institute, and it also set Class 12 as the maximum qualification. That ceiling matters, so candidates above Class 12 should check the advertisement before applying. Alongside the school qualification, candidates must hold a minimum one-year diploma in Cooking or Culinary Arts from a recognised institute. The post is listed as Junior Court Attendant (Cooking).</p>
<p>All qualifications must be held as on 1 October 2026, the date the court fixed for counting eligibility. The age limit is 18 to 27 years as on the same date, with the usual relaxations for SC, ST and OBC (non-creamy layer) candidates, candidates with disabilities and ex-servicemen. The court's roundup also records a higher ceiling for widows and for divorced or judicially separated women who have not remarried: up to 35 years, rising to 38 years for OBC candidates and 40 years for SC and ST candidates in that group. Category-wise vacancy figures have not been stated in the summaries checked. Admission to every stage is provisional, pending document verification.</p>
<h2>Pay, fee and the selection test</h2>
<p>The 130 posts sit in Pay Level 3, from Rs. 21,700 to Rs. 69,100, with a starting basic pay of Rs. 21,700 plus allowances. The application fee is Rs. 1,500 for General candidates and Rs. 750 for SC, ST, OBC (non-creamy layer), ex-servicemen and candidates with disabilities, payable online through the UCO Bank gateway. The fee is not refundable, so confirm eligibility before paying.</p>
<p>Selection runs in two stages. First comes a 100-question objective test lasting one and a half hours, with 25 questions each on general intelligence and reasoning, numerical aptitude, general English and general awareness. Candidates must score 50 marks to qualify, and those who qualify move to an interview carrying 25 marks. The court has not announced an exam date in the roundup summary checked, so candidates should watch sci.gov.in for the test schedule. With the qualifying mark fixed at 50, preparation should be spread evenly across all four sections.</p>
<h2>Two companion recruitments in the same window</h2>
<p>The Junior Court Attendant window opens alongside two other Supreme Court recruitments, and candidates should not confuse them. The court is also recruiting 15 Court Assistant (Technical Assistant-cum-Programmer) posts in Group B, at Pay Level 7 with a starting basic pay of Rs. 44,900, with applications open from 3 November 2026 to 24 November 2026. <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/supreme-court-court-assistant-programmer-15-posts-20261103">Growentix already covered that recruitment separately</a>; it needs a computer science degree and software experience, not the cooking diploma. The third recruitment, for 20 Court Master (Shorthand) posts at Pay Level 11 (starting basic Rs. 67,700), closes earlier, on 15 November 2026, and needs a bachelor's degree, shorthand at 120 words per minute and five years as a private secretary or stenographer. Together the three recruitments total 165 posts, each with its own advertisement, and a form for one post does not count for another.</p>
<p>The same distinction applies to the court's <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/supreme-court-junior-court-assistant-250-posts-20261024">earlier Junior Court Assistant recruitment</a>, a different post. This one is a Group C non-clerical cooking post in the Registry, and the cooking diploma is its clearest marker. Earlier applications do not carry over, so candidates must file afresh if they meet its conditions.</p>
<h2>How to apply, and what to do next</h2>
<p>Apply only through the links on the Supreme Court's recruitment page at <a href="https://www.sci.gov.in/">sci.gov.in</a>, as set out in the <a href="https://news.aglasem.com/supreme-court-recruitment-2026-junior-court-attendant-court-assistant-court-master/">AglaSem roundup of the three advertisements</a>. There is no offline form, so any agent offering one, or asking for payment outside the official gateway, should be treated as a fraud risk. Genuine recruitment never asks candidates to pay an agent; the only payment is the prescribed fee. Keep copies of the submitted form, the fee receipt and advertisement No. F.3/2026-SC (RC).</p>
<p>Before submitting, check four things: your Class 10 certificate and cooking diploma are from recognised institutes and held as on 1 October 2026; your age falls within 18 to 27 years on that date, or within the relaxed ceiling for your category; the maximum-qualification rule does not disqualify you; and you file early, not on 25 November 2026, when server load and payment delays are most likely. A form incomplete or unpaid at 11:55 PM on 25 November 2026 will not be accepted.</p>
<p><small>Sources: Supreme Court of India advertisements No. F.3/2026-SC (RC) of 15 October 2026, No. F.6/2026/Tech.Post/SC(RC) of 12 October 2026 and No. F.6/11/CM(S)/2026-SC (RC) of 15 October 2026, and the Recruitment Cell notice of 2 November 2026, as reported by AglaSem News on 3 November 2026; qualification summaries from FreeJobAlert and ExamNeed.</small></p>$$,
'',
ARRAY['https://news.aglasem.com/supreme-court-recruitment-2026-junior-court-attendant-court-assistant-court-master/', 'https://www.sci.gov.in/'],
NOW(), NOW(), NOW())
